00:20:49,461 --> 00:20:52,000
of mountains.

240
00:20:52,001 --> 00:20:57,240
It meant the industrialization of mines, big money,

241
00:20:57,241 --> 00:21:00,040
heavy labor, and massive machinery.

242
00:21:07,360 --> 00:21:17,240
In 1850, gold and silver were discovered at the Comstock Lode in the mountains of Nevada,

243
00:21:17,241 --> 00:21:26,480
setting the stage for revolutionary improvements in underground mining.

244
00:21:26,481 --> 00:21:32,300
Philip Deitersheimer, a mining engineer at the Comstock Lode, devised the first reliable

245
00:21:32,301 --> 00:21:35,000
means of supporting deep tunnels.

246
00:21:35,001 --> 00:21:42,000
Known as square-set timbering, the system of interlocking wood beams was so sturdy that

247
00:21:42,001 --> 00:21:46,560
the collapse of any one section would not trigger a domino effect.

248
00:21:46,561 --> 00:21:53,620
This meant now the shafts could go much deeper, and they did on the Comstock load.

249
00:21:53,621 --> 00:21:58,900
They went down to 1,000 feet, 1,200 feet, 1,500 feet.

250
00:21:58,901 --> 00:22:04,400
And after this, it was used throughout the West, in fact throughout the world.

251
00:22:04,401 --> 00:22:11,400
Deitersheimer's ingenuity allowed Comstock miners to exploit the rich core of Mount Davidson.

252
00:22:15,400 --> 00:22:22,400
For 30 years beginning in 1859, the Comstock produced $400 million in silver and gold.

253
00:22:25,400 --> 00:22:30,400
But yet again, more efficient mining meant environmental devastation.

254
00:22:30,401 --> 00:22:39,880
Square-set timbering devoured vast swaths of western trees, 80 million board feet of

255
00:22:39,881 --> 00:22:43,580
timber and lumber each year.

256
00:22:43,581 --> 00:22:48,520
Before long, the eastern slope of the Sierra Nevada was stripped bare for a stretch of

257
00:22:48,521 --> 00:22:55,520
100 miles.

258
00:22:55,521 --> 00:22:58,840
Skilled miners from distant lands flocked to the American West,

259
00:22:59,460 --> 00:23:01,700
bringing with them a cacophony of languages.

260
00:23:04,120 --> 00:23:07,360
The deeper the mines went, the hotter they got.

261
00:23:08,260 --> 00:23:13,360
Shafts on the Comstock load regularly reached a blistering 125 degrees.

262
00:23:14,360 --> 00:23:20,320
As a result, miners received a daily allotment of 95 pounds of ice per man

263
00:23:20,321 --> 00:23:23,660
and were forced to take breaks every half hour.

264
00:23:25,520 --> 00:23:36,940
It was hellish work, not suited for everyone.

265
00:23:37,940 --> 00:23:42,000
No one knew their way around a mine better than the men of Cornwall,

266
00:23:42,520 --> 00:23:45,260
who had a long heritage of tin and coal mining.

267
00:23:46,720 --> 00:23:49,260
Their industriousness inspired the maxim

268
00:23:49,261 --> 00:23:51,900
that wherever there's a hole in the ground,

269
00:23:52,320 --> 00:23:54,400
you'll find a Cornishman at the bottom of it.

270
00:23:55,520 --> 00:24:03,520
Their lore showed a healthy respect for supernatural forces.

271
00:24:03,521 --> 00:24:09,080
Cornishmen could also be hard-headed scientists, and they pioneered the use of steam engines

272
00:24:09,081 --> 00:24:12,740
to pump water from mine shafts.

273
00:24:12,741 --> 00:24:18,800
In California and Nevada, the water table is between two and three hundred feet deep,

274
00:24:18,801 --> 00:24:23,120
and any shaft below that encounters subterranean reservoirs.

275
00:24:23,121 --> 00:24:31,580
The Cornish pump could drain 18,000 gallons of water per hour and operated on some mines

276
00:24:31,581 --> 00:24:39,680
24 hours a day for decades.

277
00:24:39,681 --> 00:24:47,180
In the 1870s, a series of inventions greatly increased the efficiency of mining and the

278
00:24:47,181 --> 00:24:48,180
dangers.

279
00:24:48,181 --> 00:24:53,340
For thousands of years, gold seekers had used black powder to blast through to their treasure.

280
00:24:55,980 --> 00:25:01,820
The job of powder men paid relatively well, but it was also highly dangerous.

281
00:25:03,460 --> 00:25:07,580
Dynamite replaced black powder, quadrupling explosive power.

282
00:25:08,960 --> 00:25:12,040
With it came noxious fumes that could suffocate miners.

283
00:25:12,041 --> 00:25:23,040
And while dynamite is usually stable, when unexploded charges are placed in tightly drilled holes, removing them becomes a risky maneuver.

284
00:25:27,220 --> 00:25:36,320
In the 1880s, the invention of compressed air drills seemed like a godsend, freeing miners from backbreaking manual drilling.

285
00:25:37,820 --> 00:25:41,740
But the new drills soon became known as widow makers.

286
00:25:42,040 --> 00:25:51,360
As they cut into rock, they stirred up clouds of razor-sharp silica dust that caused the lung disease silicosis.

287
00:26:00,180 --> 00:26:05,680
Hundreds died before a safer water flush drill was introduced in the 1890s.

288
00:26:05,681 --> 00:26:16,800
Drilling deep shafts presented many complications and inspired bold solutions.

289
00:26:16,801 --> 00:26:25,500
At the Comstock load, an engineering marvel was devised that carried away excess water and toxic air.

290
00:26:27,900 --> 00:26:35,460
Adolph Sutro came up with an idea, a way to ventilate these deep shafts and the way to drain away water.

291
00:26:35,680 --> 00:26:39,620
and that was to tunnel into Mount Davidson from the side.

292
00:26:39,880 --> 00:26:42,260
This would mean tunneling almost five miles.

293
00:26:43,000 --> 00:26:45,340
It was something like finding a needle in the haystack

294
00:26:45,341 --> 00:26:49,120
to be able to hit these shafts from the side of the mountain.

295
00:26:49,720 --> 00:26:53,100
Nonetheless, Sutro did it, but it took him about a decade.

296
00:27:01,060 --> 00:27:05,200
Throughout the West, mines were becoming larger and more sophisticated.

297
00:27:05,680 --> 00:27:12,680
But on the eve of the 20th century, the last great gold rush would expose novice prospectors

298
00:27:12,681 --> 00:27:17,680
to some of nature's harshest conditions.

299
00:27:17,681 --> 00:27:28,680
A man who worked 10 years in the Western gold mines faced a one in seven chance of death.

300
00:27:28,681 --> 00:27:33,680
Gold Mines will return on Modern Marvels.

301
00:27:38,680 --> 00:27:44,380
The Yukon River in far western Canada is the fifth largest in North America.

302
00:27:45,440 --> 00:27:47,700
Among its many tributaries is the Klondike.

303
00:27:49,600 --> 00:27:55,120
Local Indians had long noticed yellow metal in streams and ignored it as useless to them.

304
00:27:55,121 --> 00:28:00,120
In 1896, news of a golden bonanza hit the front pages,

305
00:28:00,220 --> 00:28:02,960
and the rush was on.

306
00:28:03,060 --> 00:28:06,960
The stampede into the Klondike in the late 1890s

307
00:28:07,060 --> 00:28:11,880
was the last chance for those who had missed the rush

308
00:28:11,980 --> 00:28:17,480
to California in 49, the rush to Nevada in 59,

309
00:28:17,580 --> 00:28:21,680
the rush to Arizona in the early 80s.

310
00:28:21,681 --> 00:28:25,760
early 80s, all these great rushes had come and gone.

311
00:28:25,761 --> 00:28:33,480
And now there was one last chance for those to take part in a great adventure.

312
00:28:38,200 --> 00:28:43,720
The Yukon had long, frigid winters, with temperatures plunging to 50 below.

313
00:28:45,180 --> 00:28:50,920
The Canadian Mounties required anyone entering the goldfields to carry a ton of supplies.

314
00:28:51,680 --> 00:28:54,400
enough to give them a fighting chance of survival.

315
00:28:55,900 --> 00:29:00,480
At Chilkoot Pass, a never-ending stream of pack-laden men

316
00:29:00,481 --> 00:29:05,420
made four trips a day for ten days just for their personal gear.

317
00:29:10,080 --> 00:29:15,180
Remarkably, some would also transport machinery weighing many thousands of pounds.

318
00:29:16,140 --> 00:29:20,080
There were large circular dredges with a series of rotating buckets

319
00:29:20,081 --> 00:29:22,280
to scoop out dirt for treatment.

320
00:29:24,120 --> 00:29:26,060
They had to be disassembled,

321
00:29:26,540 --> 00:29:29,240
hauled in pieces up mountains and over rapids,

322
00:29:29,600 --> 00:29:31,680
and reassembled on site.

323
00:29:33,280 --> 00:29:34,460
It's amazing.

324
00:29:34,780 --> 00:29:37,860
Even today, there are dredges left from that era,

325
00:29:38,340 --> 00:29:41,080
and to see them in the middle of nowhere

326
00:29:41,081 --> 00:29:44,060
and the massive size and scale of these things,

327
00:29:44,440 --> 00:29:46,920
it's hard to conceive that these people

328
00:29:46,921 --> 00:29:49,240
were able to put these things there.

329
00:29:50,080 --> 00:29:54,680
For most of the year, the ground was frozen.

330
00:29:55,720 --> 00:30:00,040
Thawing even small sections of earth was an arduous and tedious process.

331
00:30:01,760 --> 00:30:03,820
The men would try to dig and build fires.

332
00:30:04,500 --> 00:30:11,720
They would try to direct jets of steam into the ground, into the ice, to thaw it out.

333
00:30:11,721 --> 00:30:16,020
Because waiting for them was millions of dollars of pay dirt.

334
00:30:16,021 --> 00:30:25,220
From 1899 to 1904, the Klondike produced $100 million in gold.

335
00:30:26,300 --> 00:30:32,560
As in California, the easy gold went quickly, and only a fortunate few struck it rich.

336
00:30:34,780 --> 00:30:40,440
Most considered themselves lucky simply to survive and to have shared a grand adventure.

337
00:30:40,441 --> 00:30:47,440
Poet Robert Service crystallized the spirit of the Yukon.

338
00:30:47,441 --> 00:30:50,440
You may recall in savage splendor

339
00:30:50,441 --> 00:30:54,440
that land that measures each man at his worth,

340
00:30:54,441 --> 00:30:59,440
and feel in memory, half fierce, half tender,

341
00:30:59,441 --> 00:31:02,440
the brotherhood of men that knew the North.

342
00:31:10,440 --> 00:31:22,280
The romance of the Yukon drew a new generation of entrepreneurs and engineers to the gold

343
00:31:22,281 --> 00:31:26,120
fields of Alaska.

344
00:31:26,121 --> 00:31:36,080
These professionals would pioneer techniques that revolutionized hard rock mining.

345
00:31:36,081 --> 00:31:43,404
ore is extremely low grade, with small deposits of gold spread over large areas.

346
00:31:43,405 --> 00:31:44,960
To be profitable,

347
00:31:44,961 --> 00:31:51,282
it must be collected and processed on an immense scale.

348
00:31:51,283 --> 00:31:56,340
By 1910, the Alaska Juno mine was detonating

349
00:31:56,341 --> 00:32:04,520
750 cases of dynamite at a time, breaking off millions of tons of rock in a single blast.

350
00:32:06,080 --> 00:32:17,840
Blessed with economy of scale, the mine prospered while recovering just one ounce of gold for

351
00:32:17,841 --> 00:32:22,460
every 22 tons of rock processed.

352
00:32:22,461 --> 00:32:26,020
Gold is not found in big nuggets or big chunks.

353
00:32:26,021 --> 00:32:30,480
Most people, if they were given a piece of ore, gold ore, would not have the slightest

354
00:32:30,481 --> 00:32:33,240
idea there's any gold in it.

355
00:32:33,241 --> 00:32:37,000
A miner can work his whole life and not see a fleck of gold.

356
00:32:44,320 --> 00:32:46,940
Once collected, the ore went through a multistage

357
00:32:46,941 --> 00:32:47,880
refining process.

358
00:32:59,140 --> 00:33:02,800
First, slabs of rock were crushed into manageable chunks.

359
00:33:03,240 --> 00:33:15,780
Then great stamps weighing up to 1,200 pounds a piece pulverized the stones as fine as table

360
00:33:15,781 --> 00:33:16,481
salt.

361
00:33:23,140 --> 00:33:31,620
The powdered ore was mixed with water and fed downhill to amalgamation tables where it

362
00:33:31,621 --> 00:33:36,760
was sprinkled with chemicals, first mercury, later cyanide,

363
00:33:36,761 --> 00:33:38,400
that act to separate out the gold.

364
00:33:42,260 --> 00:33:45,640
Scale was of great importance, economy of scale

365
00:33:45,641 --> 00:33:49,800
and profitably operating these gold mines.

366
00:33:49,801 --> 00:33:54,100
Treadwell had 960 stamps pounding away,

367
00:33:54,101 --> 00:33:58,180
363 days a year, the biggest stamp mill ever built

368
00:33:58,181 --> 00:33:59,180
in the history of mining.

369
00:33:59,181 --> 00:34:00,720
And these were used throughout the world

370
00:34:00,721 --> 00:34:03,260
at this point in time.

371
00:34:03,261 --> 00:34:05,860
Stamp mills generated a tremendous roar

372
00:34:05,861 --> 00:34:09,200
that could be heard a mile away.

373
00:34:09,201 --> 00:34:11,600
They operated round the clock and depended

374
00:34:11,601 --> 00:34:15,060
on cheap, reliable sources of power.

375
00:34:15,061 --> 00:34:17,980
A series of hydroelectric plants were constructed,

376
00:34:17,981 --> 00:34:20,800
and the heavy rains of southeast Alaska put to work.

377
00:34:23,540 --> 00:34:26,020
The hydroelectric dam at Annex Creek

378
00:34:26,021 --> 00:34:29,140
drew its water from a unique source,

379
00:34:29,141 --> 00:34:31,740
a tunnel driven beneath the bottom of the lake.

380
00:34:33,640 --> 00:34:35,880
This tunnel went back 1,500 feet

381
00:34:35,881 --> 00:34:39,280
and then up 80 feet to the bottom of the lake,

382
00:34:39,420 --> 00:34:41,880
which happens to be 150 feet below the surface of the water.

383
00:34:42,480 --> 00:34:44,780
They blasted a hole through the bottom of this lake,

384
00:34:44,840 --> 00:34:47,520
and the water would literally flow out the bottom like a bathtub.

385
00:34:48,240 --> 00:34:51,260
It would go down this drain, which in this case was a tunnel,

386
00:34:51,760 --> 00:34:55,880
then into a pipeline 2 1⁄2 miles down to Tidewater,

387
00:34:56,000 --> 00:34:57,200
where the power plant was.

388
00:34:59,140 --> 00:35:02,780
With their large-scale machinery and inventive technology,

389
00:35:03,520 --> 00:35:07,260
the Alaskan mines were precursors to the modern era of gold mining.

390
00:35:11,020 --> 00:35:16,960
In the 1970s, rising gold prices would bring forth gigantic new mines

391
00:35:16,961 --> 00:35:20,640
that pushed the limits of man and nature.

392
00:35:20,641 --> 00:35:33,860
During the summer of 1898, more than 7,000 boats sailed down the Yukon River to the boomtown of Dawson City.

393
00:35:35,280 --> 00:35:37,640
Gold Mines will return on Modern Marvels.

394
00:35:37,641 --> 00:35:51,420
For much of the 20th century, the value of gold was fixed at $35 an ounce.

395
00:35:52,700 --> 00:35:59,500
In 1968, the United States deregulated the price and gold skyrocketed.

396
00:36:01,140 --> 00:36:07,620
Contemporary prices of roughly $300 an ounce have inspired immensely ambitious and expensive

397
00:36:07,621 --> 00:36:09,620
to build massive new mines.

398
00:36:14,620 --> 00:36:17,620
In the American West, mountains are moved

399
00:36:17,621 --> 00:36:21,620
and landscapes obliterated to make way for vast open pit mines.

400
00:36:24,620 --> 00:36:27,620
Some of these pits are two miles long and a mile wide.

401
00:36:27,621 --> 00:36:29,620
I mean, they're massive.

402
00:36:29,621 --> 00:36:32,620
The scale of these pits are extremely impressive,

403
00:36:32,621 --> 00:36:34,620
and they're very deep, you know,

404
00:36:34,621 --> 00:36:36,620
600, 700 feet deep in some of them.

405
00:36:37,620 --> 00:36:44,380
You see these huge haul trucks where the tires on them are, say, 12 feet in diameter.

406
00:36:44,381 --> 00:36:47,140
And you look at them in the bottom of the pit when you're standing at the top,

407
00:36:47,200 --> 00:36:51,980
and they look like little toy trucks coming up the spiral road,

408
00:36:52,500 --> 00:36:54,900
hauling the ore up to the surface.

409
00:37:00,600 --> 00:37:03,220
After great loads are collected from the pits,

410
00:37:03,221 --> 00:37:09,460
cyanide is sprinkled over rocks to efficiently dissolve out microscopic flecks of gold.

411
00:37:09,461 --> 00:37:19,900
Cyanide can contaminate surrounding soil and so its residue is stored in special wells lined

412
00:37:19,901 --> 00:37:21,626
with protective plastic.

413
00:37:21,627 --> 00:37:25,940
These containers are closely monitored for signs of leakage.

414
00:37:25,941 --> 00:37:36,380
open pits typically yield one ounce of gold from 40 tons of rock enough to turn a profit at current

415
00:37:36,381 --> 00:37:47,480
prices modern minds are essentially giant assembly lines that must maintain balanced operation

416
00:37:49,580 --> 00:37:55,580
a mine that produces more ore than could be transported or processed is needlessly wasteful

417
00:37:55,581 --> 00:38:03,580
Computer simulations of changing scenarios, adding a truck here, subtracting 50 miners there,

418
00:38:04,240 --> 00:38:07,600
ensure that resources are allotted with maximum efficiency.

419
00:38:09,540 --> 00:38:15,280
The Leher mine, which is a new gold mine located on an island near Papua New Guinea,

420
00:38:15,740 --> 00:38:21,840
was the first mine in the world to be totally designed via a computer simulation and animation model.

421
00:38:21,841 --> 00:38:30,140
The model was used for more than 150 of the what-if scenarios that the engineers were able to pose.

422
00:38:31,760 --> 00:38:38,320
They did this back in Bristol, England, for the mine that was halfway around the world near Papua New Guinea.

423
00:38:41,080 --> 00:38:45,460
Building a modern mine requires years of analysis, planning, and drilling,

424
00:38:45,461 --> 00:38:50,460
and costs between $200 million and $1 billion

425
00:38:50,461 --> 00:38:53,060
before realizing any income.

426
00:38:56,640 --> 00:38:59,660
Exploration geologists are under intense pressure

427
00:38:59,661 --> 00:39:03,160
to locate rich areas that will eventually pay off.

428
00:39:08,060 --> 00:39:11,720
For underground sites, the most reliable guides

429
00:39:11,721 --> 00:39:15,000
are diamond drills that can recover core samples

430
00:39:15,001 --> 00:39:17,500
from thousands of feet below the surface.

431
00:39:24,340 --> 00:39:27,800
The deepest and richest gold mines in the world today

432
00:39:27,801 --> 00:39:29,920
are the South African mines.

433
00:39:31,820 --> 00:39:35,340
More than two miles deep, their construction demands

434
00:39:35,341 --> 00:39:39,300
a mix of meticulous instrumentation and brute force.

435
00:39:41,280 --> 00:39:44,280
During one of my trips underground in South Africa,

436
00:39:44,281 --> 00:39:48,020
We walked for a mile or two looking at everything,

437
00:39:48,180 --> 00:39:52,220
and I looked up and saw a sign hanging over the top of the drift.

438
00:39:52,400 --> 00:39:54,260
You are now leaving South Africa.

439
00:39:54,380 --> 00:39:56,800
We were entering another country deep underground.

440
00:39:57,680 --> 00:40:02,480
The surveyors were so exact, they were able to spot the exact inch

441
00:40:02,481 --> 00:40:04,160
where you're leaving the country.

442
00:40:07,160 --> 00:40:09,620
At the beginning of each eight-hour shift,

443
00:40:09,621 --> 00:40:15,320
Groups of 150 miners reach the bottom after a swift, 10-minute plunge.

444
00:40:19,900 --> 00:40:25,920
The elevator runs constantly until perhaps 2,000 men have entered an alien land.

445
00:40:28,540 --> 00:40:31,580
South African mines are a study in contrast.

446
00:40:33,360 --> 00:40:35,480
Ultramodern technology constructs them,

447
00:40:35,680 --> 00:40:38,860
and traditional backbreaking labor keeps them running.

448
00:40:39,620 --> 00:40:47,500
Giant air conditioning systems on the surface cool water, which is then pumped into the

449
00:40:47,501 --> 00:40:51,620
mine to reduce air temperature.

450
00:40:51,621 --> 00:40:58,500
Once heated, the water is brought back out again, and the process is repeated.

451
00:40:58,501 --> 00:41:07,620
The air conditioning system of a single mine could cool a city of 100,000 people.

452
00:41:07,621 --> 00:41:21,621
Miners receive special training in walking, crawling, and drilling in claustrophobic conditions.

453
00:41:21,780 --> 00:41:27,740
They hail from a variety of African tribes with different languages and rely on universal

454
00:41:27,741 --> 00:41:35,260
visual signals developed especially for underground mines.

455
00:41:35,261 --> 00:41:41,800
There are even light signals that don't require words or enunciation.

456
00:41:41,801 --> 00:41:46,800
For example, the twisting of the head and rotating is no.

457
00:41:46,801 --> 00:41:52,300
And a lot of it, if you twist a lot, it means there's danger.

458
00:41:52,301 --> 00:41:56,820
Spontaneous rock bursts are considered a kind of mini earthquake.

459
00:41:56,821 --> 00:42:01,580
And a single mine typically registers 700 such seismic events each month.

460
00:42:05,260 --> 00:42:17,020
After dynamite blasts have altered the stress levels, rocks will explode with body piercing

461
00:42:17,021 --> 00:42:23,060
force.

462
00:42:23,061 --> 00:42:29,020
Combustible methane gas, a traditional mining hazard, can occur in deep mines.

463
00:42:29,021 --> 00:42:34,020
In response to recent accidents, naked flames have been banned underground.

464
00:42:37,020 --> 00:42:46,020
The gold mine where I worked had a methane explosion due to the mixture of air and methane and a light.

465
00:42:48,020 --> 00:42:54,020
This methane explosion killed nine miners underground.

466
00:42:54,021 --> 00:42:59,020
Research is now underway to determine if even deeper gold

467
00:42:59,021 --> 00:43:01,020
mines are feasible.

468
00:43:01,021 --> 00:43:05,020
Oil wells have been dug as deep as five miles.

469
00:43:05,021 --> 00:43:10,020
The question is whether humans can survive and work at such

470
00:43:10,021 --> 00:43:11,020
depths.

471
00:43:11,021 --> 00:43:15,020
If so-called ultra-deep mines are built, they will likely be

472
00:43:15,021 --> 00:43:18,020
manned by robot miners.

473
00:43:18,021 --> 00:43:25,020
In a world of change and uncertainty, gold is eternal.

